A||||10||Bens88GT||Automatic tranny THUD... is this normal?||||9||
Z||000000||Bens88GT||02-20-2000||08:27 PM||||My bone-stock 88GT with 3-speed auto makes a very loud, hard THUD everytime I put it into drive. It doesn't do this going into reverse or park. Many times now, but not all the time, it has made the thud even harder and stalled the engine at a light (only after engine is warmed up, and more often right after hard acceleration).<P>It would stall the engine as I stopped at the light (kinda like a standard would if you didn't push the clutch in). I could sit there and start it right up again (in neutral), then when I eased it into drive it would make that godawful loud thud and stall again. It would do this 5 times in a row, sometimes more. The only solution was to let some time pass, like a couple minutes, then it would work right again (still thud but not as hard, and wouldn't stall).<P>Question: What the **** is going on? Is my tranny about to die, or is this loud thud normal? I've never driven any other automatic Fieros. Thanks for any help!<P>-Ben||||reg||9
Z||000001||theogre||02-20-2000||08:53 PM||||hot stall on TH125c automatic is most often the torque converter lockup solinoid. there is an electrical connector on the front of the tranny. unplug it and see if the hot stall problem stops.<P>the clunk is likely a broken mount or failed Torque reaction strut(dogbone).||||reg||1
Z||000002||batboy||02-20-2000||08:54 PM||||No, it's not suppose to make those noises. I'd first check the dogbone and the lower motor and tranny mounts. I'll almost bet the dogbone is totally shot. Get one of those ultimate dogbones with the poly bushings from the Fiero Store. If all those are in great shape, then start worrying, it might just be the tranny.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Bens88GT||02-20-2000||11:19 PM||||Hey, thanks guys. You were right on both counts, it appears. I pulled that wire and so far the hot stalling has stopped. And I looked at the dogbone, and its bushings look like hamburger. Lots of play. Thanks again.<P>-Ben||||reg||7
Z||000004||theogre||02-21-2000||09:26 AM||||now that you know it's the TC solinoid go to GM and get a new one. they aren't very expensive and you can replace it yourself. It lives under the side cover of the tranny.<P>Driving without it will likely hurt gas milage and will make the tranny run hotter.<P>Try to get a side cover gasket and use one of the paint or spray sealers on it. If you can't get a gasket use RTV VERY carfully or you could get peices of the stuff inside the tranny and that can cause problems. which ever type sealer you end up using everything has to be comletely clean before applying the sealer.||||reg||1
Z||000005||burdell1||02-23-2000||09:33 AM||||Where is that wire for the solenoid? I know you said it's on the transmission, but where is that? I'd assume I'd have to go underneath the car, but I have no idea.||||reg||5
Z||000006||theogre||02-23-2000||09:48 AM||||it's on the front side of the tranny. it can be reached from the top. you don't need to get under it. it's the only wire going to the tranny not bolted to the bell housing.||||reg||1
Z||000007||burdell1||02-23-2000||11:09 AM||||This might sound like a dumb question but what does the tranny look like? Do you have a picture of it that you can post?||||reg||1
Z||000008||burdell1||03-03-2000||12:56 PM||||Doesn't anyone know?||||reg||1
Z||000009||Carrolles||03-03-2000||01:24 PM||||Standing at the trunk looking down at the engine you will see the transmission (tranny) bolted to the lower left side of the engine.  It will have the shift calbes attached.  You will also see an axel with a black rubber corrugated CV (constant velocity) boot  sticking out of the right and left sides going to the wheels.<P>Below your alternator (right side below the dog bone strut) you will see a part of the transmission protruding to the right.<P>There should be a transmission fluid dip stick somewhere (I'm not sure exactly where because I have a 4 spd in my car).||||reg||1
Z||000010||Raydar||03-03-2000||01:58 PM||||Burdell... Find the rubber intake duct that goes between the air filter housing and the throttle body. The air filter housing is that round canister-looking thing that's in the very front corner of the engine compartment, on the driver's side of the car. The transmission is about 2-2.5 feet below this duct, on the back (left side) of the engine. If you look on the front side of the transmission (toward the firewall), you will see a square connector that plugs in to the transmission. That is the TCC connector. If you unplug it, the TCC wil not be able to lock. <BR>Several members have stated that leaving this unplugged will cause your transmission to wear out more rapidly. In the cases I am familiar with, I have not found this to be true. One that I know of (in a Lumina) has been unplugged for 5-6 years, and about 100,000 miles(!) with no failure.  Same trans., heavier car. <BR>Your gas mileage *will* suffer, a bit. <BR>Hope this helps. ||||reg||1
